-- lib/repeats.cub — every text indexed by all of its repeated token sequences (the workflow's
-- index-every-repeated-sequence, Avik 2026-11-16 14:28 UTC: "index all text by token n-grams for every n
-- ... really simple algorithms"), built 2026-11-17 for PR #10, item 3. What is here, each with its proof
-- for every text:
--   tokens        tokenize: a run of word bytes (letters, digits, _ -, every byte of a multi-byte
--                 character) or one other byte, spaces between; a token is its bytes, so two tokens
--                 are the same token exactly when their bytes are (identity by content).
--                 tokenize-content: the tokens joined are the text without its spaces.
--   order         word-order, lex-order, pair-order: the machine's order on words is total, and a total
--                 order on elements is one on their lists and on pairs; suffixes are ordered
--                 lexicographically by tokens, tokens by bytes.
--   suffix array  suffix-array: every suffix with the point it starts at, merge-sorted (lib/sort.cub's
--                 merge, halving, any fuel). suffix-array-sorted: sorted; suffix-array-permutation:
--                 each suffix with its point as often as in the text.
--   LCP           lcp-array: each suffix's longest common prefix with the next. lcp-array-ok: that
--                 prefix is common to the two (lcp-common) and can go no further: past it one ends or
--                 the next tokens differ (lcp-stops).
--   query         earlier-match: at a point, the longest token sequence starting there that also
--                 starts earlier (the Lempel-Ziv 1977 step; overlap allowed), its length and point.
--                 earlier-match-max: no earlier point has a longer one; earlier-match-at: the length is
--                 zero or found at an earlier point with exactly that common prefix.
--   equivalence   a class representative per token (representative: declared pairs); equivalent is an
--                 equivalence relation; index-upto indexes the representatives, so every theorem
--                 holds of it, and an exact match there is a match up to the equivalence.
-- The suffix array is sorted by comparing suffixes token by token, the LCP taken pair by pair: the
-- algorithms whose correctness is proved here for every text. Prefix doubling (Manber and Myers 1990)
-- and Kasai's LCP (2001) bound the work by the text's length whatever its repeats; they are not built
-- here. The measured run over the source is experiments/repeats/run.cub, its numbers in the node.
import "core.cub"
import "sort.cub"
